ould be  if our rules look like this then there's  some invariant of the state which is  that there's always exactly one  of the green daughter the blue dot  and finally um i guess i'm a little bit  out of time but i  i wanted to give some historical context  to this  i didn't invent linear logic programming  i just i like it and i'm trying to  extend the ideas and use it for things  um but linear logic was uh invented in  1987 by johnny gerard prolog's been  around  since 72 and a lot of this work is  inspired by  lf which stands for logical framework so  the idea of having like a proof system  um and these ideas kind of all  influenced each other actually i  realized after the fact that this is  kind of the same form as the like  narrative causality graph that i had  drawn before um  so so these things all fed into into the  idea of clf which is the formal system  itself which is the implementation and  there's me i'm interested in narrative  interactive systems  i'm trying to put this all together  so i hope you enjoyed that and there is  code for self available that you can use  and once i get the internet working  again i'm gonna put the examples from  this talk there  and you can find me there thanks very  [Music]  much

